# AL File Header — License Comment

Every `.al` file begins with a 4-line comment block declaring copyright and license. This is what Microsoft uses in every file of `microsoft/BCApps`:

```al
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation. All rights reserved.
// Licensed under the MIT License. See License.txt in the project root for license information.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
```

The block sits at the **very top of the file**, before the `namespace` declaration. No blank line before it; one blank line after it.

## Publisher line is configurable

When the consuming repo declares a different publisher convention, replace line 2 to match. The structure of the block (4 lines, `//` prefix, 96-character horizontal rule on lines 1 and 4) stays the same.

Common variants by publisher:

```al
// Verosoft Design INC. (TAG)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Copyright (c) Verosoft Design INC. All rights reserved.
// Licensed under the MIT License. See License.txt in the project root for license information.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
```

```al
// Single-vendor proprietary
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Copyright (c) <Publisher> <Year>. All rights reserved.
// Confidential — distribution prohibited.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
```

The agent can detect the right publisher line by:

1. Reading an existing `.al` file in the repo and matching its header.
2. Reading `app.json` → `publisher`.
3. Asking the user when the repo is brand-new and no existing header is detectable.

## Detection heuristics for review

Flag any `.al` file that:

- Has no comment block at all in the first 6 lines.
- Has a comment block that mentions a different publisher than the rest of the repo (likely copy-paste error).
- Has a license declaration that contradicts the repo's `LICENSE` file (e.g. file claims MIT but repo `LICENSE` is proprietary).

## Opting out

For repos that intentionally do NOT carry per-file license headers (e.g. internal-only PTEs where the repo-level `LICENSE` is considered sufficient), opt out by adding a more specific rule in the consuming repo's `.cursor/rules/`:

```yaml
---
description: Disable the global AL license header rule.
alwaysApply: true
globs: **/*.al
---

# This repo does not use per-file license headers.
# The global al-license-header rule should be ignored.
```

The local rule wins on conflict.

